INSERT INTO table2 (c13, c50, c29, c47, c12) 
  VALUES ('badkey', 'dummy', 1, 1, 499);

/*
Query Q1 (using table3 as the driving table) will only find the valid join between table3 and table2 for 'key1' and return 'Result1'.

Query Q2 (using table2 as the driving table filtered by c12 = 499) will consider both rows from table2; however, when it attempts to join, only the row with c13 = 'key1' finds a partner in table3. In some SQL engines the row with 'badkey' might be discarded by the inner join, but the fact that the filtering happens on a different table can affect performance and (in outer join variants) even the result.

This contrived counterexample illustrates that reordering the join and placing the filter on a different table (even when nominally equivalent under full referential integrity) can lead to different outcomes if integrity is not perfect.
*/

