INSERT INTO table4 (c7, c6) VALUES ('X', '+');
INSERT INTO table4 (c7, c6) VALUES ('Y', '-');

/*
In the intended counterexample the two queries are modified to use a LEFT JOIN.

Query1 (using LEFT JOIN with the table4 predicate in the ON clause):

  SELECT DISTINCT T1.c7
  FROM table2 AS T1
  LEFT JOIN table4 AS T2
    ON T1.c7 = T2.c7 AND T2.c6 = '-'
  WHERE T1.c4 = '-';

This returns both 'X' and 'Y' because the row for 'X' does not match any table4 row (T2 is NULL) but is not filtered by the WHERE clause.

Query2 (using LEFT JOIN with the table4 predicate in the WHERE clause):

  SELECT DISTINCT T1.c7
  FROM table2 AS T1
  LEFT JOIN table4 AS T2
    ON T1.c7 = T2.c7
  WHERE T1.c4 = '-' AND T2.c6 = '-';

This returns only 'Y' because the row for 'X' fails the T2.c6 = '-' test in the WHERE clause (since T2.c6 is NULL).

This counterexample shows that (when using an OUTER JOIN) the position of predicates matters and the two query formulations are not semantically equivalent.  Note that while the original queries use an INNER JOIN (and for that join type the two WHERE orders are logically equivalent), the example illustrates a common pitfall when predicates “leak” into the join condition via reordering.
*/

