/*
Now consider the following outer query which uses the two alternate subqueries Q1 and Q2:

SELECT outerT.c1,
       (SELECT COUNT(T.c1) FROM table1 AS T WHERE T.c7 = 'TR005') AS cnt_q1,
       (SELECT COUNT(c1) FROM table1 WHERE c7 = 'TR005') AS cnt_q2
FROM table1 AS outerT;

Under proper scoping rules the inner FROM of each subquery should bind c1 from table1, so both subqueries would return 2. However, in some SQL implementations the unqualified c1 in Q2 is resolved to the outer query’s column (outerT.c1) instead. Since outerT.c1 is nonnull, the COUNT aggregates one non‐null value per outer row, and so cnt_q2 comes out as 1 rather than 2. This difference in the results of Q1 and Q2 serves as the counterexample.
*/

