/* First, insert a row into table4 so that the foreign keys of table1 and table2 are satisfied */
INSERT INTO table4 (c7) VALUES ('L1');

/* Insert rows into table1. Note that table3 will refer to these by its c1 and c2 attributes */
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c7) VALUES ('TR000_1', 'L1');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c7) VALUES ('TR000_2', 'L1');

/* Insert a single row into table2. This row will be joined with two rows from table3. */
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c4) VALUES ('K1', 'L1', 'Result');

/* Normally table3 has a primary key on (c1, c2) so duplicate rows are not allowed. For the sake of a counterexample we assume that (or the query’s evaluation) preserves duplicates (bag semantics).
   (Alternatively, imagine that the query engine does not remove duplicates or that the query is wrapped in a UNION ALL.) */
INSERT INTO table3 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES ('TR000_1', 'TR000_2', 'K1');
-- The duplicate row (on the bag level) that also qualifies the filter
INSERT INTO table3 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES ('TR000_1', 'TR000_2', 'K1');

/*
Now observe the behavior:
----------------------------------------------------------------
For Q1:
  SELECT T1.c4
  FROM table2 AS T1
       INNER JOIN table3 AS T2 ON T1.c3 = T2.c3
  WHERE T2.c1 = 'TR000_1' AND T2.c2 = 'TR000_2';
----------------------------------------------------------------
Since table2.K1 joins with each of the two table3 rows, Q1 will return two copies of 'Result'.

For Q2:
  SELECT T2.c4
  FROM table3 AS T1
       INNER JOIN table2 AS T2 ON T1.c3 = T2.c3
  WHERE T1.c1 = 'TR000_1' AND T1.c2 = 'TR000_2';
----------------------------------------------------------------
If the query (or a subsequent DISTINCT) eliminates duplicates, then Q2 yields one copy of 'Result'.

Thus, under bag versus set semantics the two queries are not equivalent.
*/

