/* Create supplemental table that will participate in a join, thereby introducing an ambiguous column name */
CREATE TABLE table5 (
    c7 VARCHAR
);

/* Insert test data into table4 (the original table) */
INSERT INTO table4(c7, c6) VALUES ('a', '+');
INSERT INTO table4(c7, c6) VALUES ('b', '+');

/* Insert test data into table5 */
INSERT INTO table5(c7) VALUES ('x');

/*
Now consider two queries that join table4 and table5. The query Q1 qualifies its column reference and is written as follows:

  SELECT COUNT(T.c7)
  FROM table4 AS T
  JOIN table5 AS U ON U.c7 = T.c7
  WHERE T.c6 = '+';

This query counts only those rows coming from table4 (via alias T) where T.c6 = '+'.

In contrast, a query modeled on Q2 that does not qualify c7 might be written as:

  SELECT COUNT(c7)
  FROM table4
  JOIN table5 ON table5.c7 = table4.c7
  WHERE table4.c6 = '+';

In this case the unqualified c7 in the SELECT list is ambiguous. In many SQL dialects the name‐resolution rules will then (perhaps inadvertently) bind c7 to the column from table5 rather than table4. If, for instance, table5.c7 contains one non‐NULL value that matches only one of table4’s two rows, then Q2 will return 1 while Q1 returns 2. This demonstrates that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent in a larger query context where an unqualified column name may be resolved differently.

-- (For the purposes of this counterexample, we assume that the query is embedded in a larger context where the join causes the ambiguity described.)

