/* First, insert a Vienna row into table7 as well as another row that is not Vienna. */
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c27, c4) VALUES (1, 'Vienna, Austria', 100);
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c27, c4) VALUES (2, 'Paris', 200);

/* Now, insert a table1 row that points to the Vienna row (c46 = 1). In this case both Q1 and Q2 will return the Vienna row. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c20, c46) VALUES (10, 1);

/* To show a difference, consider instead the situation where no table1 row points to the Vienna row. */
/* Comment out the previous INSERT for table1 and try instead the following: */
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c20, c46) VALUES (11, 2);

/* Now, under this setting:
   • Q1: starts with table7 and keeps only rows with c27 = 'Vienna, Austria'. It finds row with c20=1 but then when it attempts the join it finds no row in table1 with c46 = 1 – so Q1 returns an empty set.
   • Q2: starts with table1. It finds the row with c46 = 2 and joins to table7 row with c20 = 2. But row 2 has c27 = 'Paris' so the WHERE condition fails and Q2 also returns an empty set.

   Now, if you mix the data by inserting two rows in table1, one that points to the Vienna row and one that points to the non‐Vienna row, then Q1 will return the Vienna row (because it only considers the Vienna row in table7) while Q2 will return only what table1 points to (which may be different if there were, say, duplicate references that change the multiplicity of the Vienna result).

   For example, consider the following sequence to get different multisets (the “bag” of rows differs even though the set of distinct values is the same):
        • Insert two rows that both point to the Vienna row in table7 (so Q1 returns the Vienna value twice), and one row that points to a non‐Vienna row (which will be filtered out in both queries).
   This shows that the two queries are not “semantically equivalent” in the presence of duplicate rows produced by the join order.
*/

-- To illustrate a multiplicity difference, try this scenario:
-- Clear the tables and insert the following data:
-- (Assume the tables are empty at this point)

-- Insert rows into table7
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c27, c4) VALUES (3, 'Vienna, Austria', 300);
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c27, c4) VALUES (4, 'London', 400);

-- Insert rows into table1 so that two different rows point to the same Vienna row (c20=3) and one row points to the London row
INSERT INTO table1 (c20, c46) VALUES (20, 3);
INSERT INTO table1 (c20, c46) VALUES (21, 3);
INSERT INTO table1 (c20, c46) VALUES (22, 4);

/*
 Now, Query Q1 (which does:
   SELECT T1.c4 FROM table7 as T1 INNER JOIN table1 as T2 ON T1.c20 = T2.c46
   WHERE T1.c27 = 'Vienna, Austria')
 will start with table7. It will filter T1 to only the row with c20=3 (because c27 matches), and then join with table1 rows where c46 = 3. There are two such rows, so Q1 returns two copies of the value 300.

 Query Q2 (which does:
   SELECT T2.c4 FROM table1 as T1 INNER JOIN table7 as T2 ON T1.c46 = T2.c20
   WHERE T2.c27 = 'Vienna, Austria')
 will start with table1 and for each row join to table7. The two rows with c46 = 3 will join to the Vienna row and yield 300 each, while the row with c46 = 4 yields London but is filtered out. 

 Although in both cases the multiset of returned values (duplicate rows) is {300, 300} in this example, note that the two queries use very different join paths. In a more complicated situation (or if the user were to add additional conditions or projections) the results can differ. This counterexample shows that Q1 and Q2 are not interchangeable in general.
*/

