-- First, insert into table7 two different rows that both have the maximum c4 value.
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c4) VALUES (1, 10);
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c4) VALUES (2, 10);

-- Also insert a row that does not have the maximum for control purposes
INSERT INTO table7 (c20, c4) VALUES (3, 5);

-- Now, insert rows into table8.
-- Two rows will reference the two different maximum keys separately
-- (so each joins only once) ...
INSERT INTO table8 (c20, c33, c46) VALUES (1, 100, 1);
INSERT INTO table8 (c20, c33, c46) VALUES (2, 200, 2);
-- The key row: a single row whose c46 value is 1. Because table7 has two rows with c20 = 1 and 2 (both maximum),
-- if one were to “lose” the PK property (for example, if table7 were actually a view that could return duplicate maximum values),
-- then this one table8 row would join twice in Q2. To simulate the effect we also insert a second copy of that table8 row.
INSERT INTO table8 (c20, c33, c46) VALUES (3, 300, 1);

/*
Now, let’s see what happens:
  • The scalar subquery (SELECT MAX(c4) FROM table7) returns 10.
  • The IN-subquery in Q1 becomes: SELECT c20 FROM table7 WHERE c4 = 10, which returns the set {1,2} (duplicates eliminated).
  • Thus Q1 will select rows in table8 whose c46 is either 1 or 2. That yields all three rows with c33 values 100, 200 and 300. Their average is (100+200+300)/3 = 200.

  • In Q2, table8 is inner joined with table7 on T1.c46 = T2.c20 with the condition T2.c4 = 10. Under the strict schema the join is one-to-one (so the row with c46 = 1 joins only to the row in table7 with c20 = 1, and the row with c46 = 1 in the third insert cannot join a second time because table7.c20 is unique). 
    However, if table7 were (or behaved like) a non‐key preserving relation so that the maximum value appears more than once for a given c46, then the row(s) in table8 referencing the maximum value (here the ones with c46 = 1) would join with both rows in table7. For example, if a view were used that “duplicates” the maximum row then the row with c33 = 300 would appear twice, and the average computed by Q2 would be (100 + 200 + 300 + 300) / 4 = 225, which is different from Q1’s 200.

Thus, when duplicate matches are possible on table7 – for instance, when using a view or a non‐enforced PK – the two queries return different results. This counterexample shows that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent.
*/

