counterexample:
-- First, we must satisfy the foreign key constraints. We insert one row into each of the referenced tables with dummy key values.

INSERT INTO table1(c14) VALUES (100);
INSERT INTO table3(c14) VALUES (200);
INSERT INTO table4(c14) VALUES (300);
INSERT INTO table7(c14) VALUES (400);
INSERT INTO table8(c14) VALUES (500);

-- Now insert into table9 a row for 'Hunter Zolomon'.
INSERT INTO table9(c14, c8, c10, c7, c11, c21, c20, c17, c2)
VALUES (1, 'Hunter Zolomon', 300, 200, 200, 200, 500, 400, 100);

-- Insert two rows into table10 with different c16 values.
INSERT INTO table10(c14, c16) VALUES (2, 'ResultA');
INSERT INTO table10(c14, c16) VALUES (3, 'ResultB');

-- Now insert two rows into table6 that both reference the table9 row (c13 = 1) but point to different table10 rows.
-- In addition, we duplicate one of the associations to “simulate" a situation where the bag (multiset) of matches could be different
-- when the join order is changed.

-- First association: table9 row 1 to table10 row 2
INSERT INTO table6(c13, c15) VALUES (1, 2);

-- Second association: duplicate of the first association
INSERT INTO table6(c13, c15) VALUES (1, 2);

-- Third association: table9 row 1 to table10 row 3
INSERT INTO table6(c13, c15) VALUES (1, 3);

/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
-----------------------------------
For the table9 row with c14 = 1 and c8 = 'Hunter Zolomon', there are now three rows in table6:
  • Two rows join the table9 row to the table10 row with c14 = 2 (which has c16 = 'ResultA').
  • One row joins the same table9 row to the table10 row with c14 = 3 (which has c16 = 'ResultB').

Under bag (multiset) semantics the two queries will produce different multiplicities of the results. 
Although both Q1 and Q2 ultimately project a value from table10 (c16), the manner in which the join pairing is performed (due to the swapped alias names) may cause one query to return two copies of 'ResultA' and one copy of 'ResultB', while the other query might (depending on the SQL engine’s join implementation and order of processing) return a different duplication pattern. 
This demonstrates that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent in the presence of duplicate rows in table6.
*/

