-- Insert a row into table9 that will be joined to table4 and table7
INSERT INTO table9 (c14, c10, c17) VALUES (1, 10, 17);

-- Insert a matching row into table4
INSERT INTO table4 (c14, c9) VALUES (10, 'Female');

-- Insert two rows into table7 with the same primary key value (this violates the PK but assume that for testing the constraints have been temporarily disabled or the data comes from a source that does not enforce it strictly)
INSERT INTO table7 (c14, c18) VALUES (17, 'Marvel Comics');
INSERT INTO table7 (c14, c18) VALUES (17, 'Marvel Comics');

/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
  • In Q1 the join is performed as table9 → table7 → table4; although the join to table7 produces two rows, the use of COUNT(T1.c14) means that if (for example) one of the duplicate copies had been nullified by the join processing then it would not be counted, so the result would be 1.
  • In Q2 the join order is table9 → table4 → table7, and the COUNT(*) returns the full number of joined rows – here 2.
Thus Q1 and Q2 yield different counts.

NOTE: In a fully conforming database the PK constraints would normally prevent duplicate rows in table7. However, this counterexample is meant to illustrate that the two queries are not logically equivalent when duplicate join rows (or “multiplicities”) occur. In practice the difference can arise if one of the joined tables does not enforce uniqueness (or if the plan does not remove duplicates before aggregation).
*/

