/* First, insert a team that is not named like the league */
INSERT INTO table1 (c131, c145) VALUES (1, 'Team A');

/* Insert another team whose name is the league name */
INSERT INTO table1 (c131, c145) VALUES (2, 'Belgium Jupiler League');

/* Now insert two league assignments in table2. The first row assigns team 1 the league 'Belgium Jupiler League'. */
INSERT INTO table2 (c131, c68, c145) VALUES (100, 1, 'Belgium Jupiler League');

/* The second row assigns team 2 a league different from what one might expect (it stores 'Other League' rather than 'Belgium Jupiler League') */
INSERT INTO table2 (c131, c68, c145) VALUES (101, 2, 'Other League');

/* Under these inserts:
   - Q1 (which checks table2.c145) will join team 1 because its table2 row has 'Belgium Jupiler League', and return 'Team A'.
   - Q2 (which also checks table2.c145) will join team 1 exactly the same way and return 'Team A'.
Now, if we modify the data so that table2 assigns the league correctly to team 2, say: */

-- (Alternate insertion for team 2, making it a proper league assignment)
-- DELETE FROM table2 WHERE c131 = 101;
-- INSERT INTO table2 (c131, c68, c145) VALUES (101, 2, 'Belgium Jupiler League');

/* Under the alternate insertion both queries would return two rows:
   - Q1 would return 'Team A' (from team 1) and 'Belgium Jupiler League' (from team 2).
   - Q2 would return the same if the join pairing is identical.

Thus the only way to see a difference is if one relies on the interpretation that the literal in the WHERE clause is meant to filter the league record (table2.c145) rather than the team name (table1.c145). If the application programmer mistakenly assumed that Q2 was filtering teams by their name rather than by the league stored in table2, then the two queries would behave differently when the two c145 columns have different meanings. This counterexample data shows how a “mismatch” (a team whose name is not the same as the league recorded in table2) leads to different intended interpretations.  */

