counterexample:
INSERT INTO table3 (c23, c15) VALUES (1, 'Bob');
INSERT INTO table3 (c23, c15) VALUES (2, 'Alice');

-- For Bob, a single lab record with c24 = 400
INSERT INTO table2 (c23, c13, c24) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 400);

-- For Alice, two lab records with the same c24 = 400
INSERT INTO table2 (c23, c13, c24) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 400);
INSERT INTO table2 (c23, c13, c24) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 400);

-- Explanation of the expected outcome:
-- Both queries will join the two patients with their lab records; the maximum (and only qualifying) c24 value is 400 in all cases. However, because there is a tie (multiple rows with c24 = 400) and the ORDER BY clause does not provide a tie‐breaking rule, the DBMS is free to return either Bob’s or Alice’s patient name. Due to the difference in the driving table (table3 in Q1 versus table2 in Q2) the arbitrary tie-break may work out differently so that Q1 returns one patient’s name (say, Bob) while Q2 returns the other (Alice). This counterexample distinguishes the two queries.

