counterexample:
-- For the purpose of testing the non–equivalence note that we must simulate a situation where table3 has duplicate rows for the same c23 value. (In a production database this would violate the PRIMARY KEY constraint, but here we assume a test database where such duplicates can occur.)

-- Insert duplicate rows into table3 (simulate dirty data)
INSERT INTO table3 (c23, c39) VALUES (1, 'M');
INSERT INTO table3 (c23, c39) VALUES (1, 'M');

-- Insert corresponding row into table2
INSERT INTO table2 (c23, c13, c32) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 10);

-- Explanation of the effect:
-- Q1 (starting from table3) will join the (logically) de-duplicated key 1 with the table2 row and yield a single qualifying value 10, so AVG = 10.
-- Q2 (starting from table2) will join the table2 row with both duplicate copies in table3, producing two rows with value 10. Although AVG(10,10) is still 10, consider what would happen if the data were more varied. For example, if there were two different table2 rows for key 1, Q2 would “over-count” the contribution from table3 duplicates and yield a different result from Q1.

-- Thus, this test data (with duplicate table3 rows) distinguishes the two query organizations.

