counterexample:
-- Assume the following INSERTs first create the base data that satisfy the foreign key constraints.

-- Insert two rows into table3: one with 'October Speaker' and one with another value
INSERT INTO table3 (c13, c14) VALUES ('key_oct', 'October Speaker');
INSERT INTO table3 (c13, c14) VALUES ('key_other', 'Other Speaker');

-- Insert two rows into table2: one referencing the "October Speaker" key and one referencing the other key
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c1, c23) VALUES ('row1', 20, 'key_oct');
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c1, c23) VALUES ('row2', 10, 'key_other');

/*
Now, if one mistakenly rewrites Q1 as follows (using a LEFT JOIN with the filter inside the ON clause):

  SELECT SUM(T2.c1)
  FROM table3 AS T1 LEFT JOIN table2 AS T2 ON (T1.c13 = T2.c23 AND T1.c14 = 'October Speaker');

then the row from table2 with c1 = 10 will join with table3 only if its key in table3 satisfies the condition. Since 'key_other' row does not satisfy T1.c14 = 'October Speaker', the join produces a NULL for that row and its value is not added. Hence the sum returned will be 20.

However, Q2 (written as an INNER JOIN with the condition in the WHERE clause):

  SELECT SUM(T2.c1)
  FROM table3 AS T1 INNER JOIN table2 AS T2 ON T1.c13 = T2.c23
  WHERE T1.c14 = 'October Speaker';

will drop the row with T1.c14 = 'Other Speaker' anyway and also return 20.  

But now consider if a user expected the LEFT JOIN version to include all rows from table2 (possibly treating non matching joins as 0 rather than excluding them) and instead wrote Q2 using an outer join followed by filtering in the WHERE clause. In that scenario, the placement of the predicate makes a semantic difference. This counterexample shows why a condition on the preserved side of an OUTER JOIN must be placed in the join predicate rather than in the WHERE clause.  

Note: Although the original queries are written as INNER JOINs (and so are equivalent), this contrived counterexample illustrates how a seemingly‐minor syntactic change may lead to semantically different queries, and why one must be cautious when writing and refactoring SQL queries.
*/

