counterexample:
-- Under the given schema (with table5.c25 referencing table7.c29) and using inner joins, any test data that obeys the constraints will cause Q1 and Q2 to return the same results. For example:

INSERT INTO table7 (c29, c19, c21, c41, c24) VALUES ('A', 'Connor', 'Hilton', 1, 'alpha');

INSERT INTO table5 (c20, c9, c35, c25) VALUES ('r1', 'Result1', 'Dues', 'A');
INSERT INTO table5 (c20, c9, c35, c25) VALUES ('r2', 'Result2', 'NotDues', 'A');

-- Q1 will join table7 row 'A' with both table5 rows but then filter out 'r2' (since its c35 isn’t 'Dues') and return only 'Result1'.
-- Q2 will start from table5, keep only row 'r1' (c35 = 'Dues'), join with table7 row 'A' (which satisfies the conditions) and return 'Result1'.

-- Thus, both queries return the identical result.

-- In summary, no valid counterexample exists over instances that satisfy the schema; the apparent difference is only notational.

