/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
  • In both Q1 and Q2 the join condition (table7.c29 = table4.c25) is satisfied because table4.c25 = 'A' and table7.c29 = 'A'.
  • In both rows in table4 the condition c7 < 50 holds (40 and 30 are both < 50).

Now, under bag (multiset) semantics each query will produce two rows with the value 100. However, if one “sets‐ifies” the result (for example, if the outer query applied DISTINCT or if the application expects set semantics), then Q1 – which conceptually starts from table7 – would yield a single value (100) while Q2 – which starts from table4 – would have produced two 100’s before duplicate removal. Thus, the two queries do not guarantee the same multiplicities, and therefore they are not semantically equivalent.

Note: In many SQL implementations the raw results (without DISTINCT) are multisets, so both queries would output 100 twice. The key point is that the two query formulations differ in how many “copies" of a table7 row may appear when joining to multiple table4 rows that satisfy the respective filter, so in a context where duplicate‐count matters (or when further aggregation is done) the two queries can behave differently.
*/

