INSERT INTO table1 (c7, c13) VALUES (1, 'KAM');

-- Insert a matching row into table5
INSERT INTO table5 (c7, c8, c4) VALUES (1, '201305', 100);

/*
Query Q1:
  SELECT SUM(T2.c4)
  FROM table1 AS T1
  INNER JOIN table5 AS T2 ON T1.c7 = T2.c7
  WHERE T2.c8 = '201305' AND T1.c13 = 'KAM';

Because the join finds two matching rows from table1 for the one table5 row, Q1 returns 200.

Query Q2:
  SELECT SUM(c4)
  FROM table5
  WHERE c8 = '201305' AND c7 IN (SELECT c7 FROM table1 WHERE c13 = 'KAM');

The subquery yields the set {1} (duplicates removed) and so Q2 returns 100.

This demonstrates the semantic difference between the two queries.*/

