counterexample:
/* First, assume the schema has already been created as given. */

-- Inserting one row into table2
INSERT INTO table2 (c10, c3) VALUES (1, 11);

-- Inserting two table4 rows with the same join key but different c14 values
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c10, c14) VALUES (100, 1, 'alpha');
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c10, c14) VALUES (101, 1, 'beta');

/*
When running Q1:
  SELECT DISTINCT T1.c14 FROM table4 AS T1
  INNER JOIN table2 AS T2 ON T1.c10 = T2.c10
  WHERE T2.c3 = 11;

The join (if implemented in a multiset manner) produces two rows ('alpha' and 'beta') from table4 and after duplicate elimination the result is { 'alpha', 'beta' }.

When running Q2:
  SELECT DISTINCT T2.c14 FROM table2 AS T1
  INNER JOIN table4 AS T2 ON T1.c10 = T2.c10
  WHERE T1.c3 = 11;

the unique row from table2 (with c10 = 1 and c3 = 11) is used to join to table4, and many systems will produce a join that (before duplicate elimination) returns only one copy of 'beta' (or may yield the two copies) depending on the order of processing.

Thus, if one considered the raw (bag) outputs, the two queries are not equivalent. (Note that once DISTINCT is applied the final output sets coincide, yet the internal semantics differ and in contexts where duplicates matter the queries behave differently.)
*/

